    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d153">

                <pb facs="0371" n="329" sameAs="frus:frus1951v04p1/pg329"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">No. 153</seg>
                </opener>
                <note rend="inline" type="source">Secretary’s Memoranda, lot 53D444,
                    Memoranda—January 1951</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Lucius D. Battle, Special Assistant to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1951-01-30">January 30, 1951</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In a meeting this afternoon preceding the meeting with Mr. Pleven, Mr. Acheson
                    said that he had talked last night to Mr. Pleven and had raised with him then
                    the “Yugoslav matter.”<anchor n="1" xml:id="d153-f1"/> It was the consensus of
                    the meeting that since we had talked with Mr. Pleven about this last night it
                    was not necessary to discuss it in the meeting this afternoon. I gather that the
                    Secretary followed the lines of the Department’s notes on this subject<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d153-f2"/> as he said that this had been omitted in the
                    discussion this morning but that he had covered the matter last night in the
                    discussion.</p>
                <p>The Secretary also said that he had spoken to Mr. Pleven last night about the
                    French proposal for consultation of the Governments of the United States, United
                    Kingdom and France. He said that he told him that this would cause much trouble.
                    The Secretary did not go into any further detail on what he had said but I am
                    confident he repeated the line which was prepared for the President’s remarks in
                    the meeting today.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d153-f1" n="1" xml:id="d153-f1-content">
                <p>No record of this conversation has been found in Department of State files.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d153-f2" n="2" xml:id="d153-f2-content">
                <p>Presumably a reference to the briefing paper prepared for the Pleven visit
                    entitled “Western Policy Towards Yugoslavia (Pleven D-6/4)”. Regarding these
                    papers, see Document 148.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
